//
// A file-scope `NAME: text = …` is a CONSTANT — it is inlined at every use, so
// there is nothing for an assignment to write to.  Saying so used to be an
// INTERNAL COMPILER ERROR rather than a diagnostic: the text-assignment arm
// intercepts before the general operator dispatch that refuses `5 = 6`, and
// handed codegen a target naming no variable, which surfaced as an
// index-out-of-bounds on variable 65535 and took the compiler down.
//
// Found while writing `lib/git` (@PLN119 arc F), which reached for exactly this
// shape for a settable working directory.  It is an easy thing to write, because
// the same declaration for an `integer` has always been refused with a message.
//
// A file of its own rather than a row in 102-expected-errors.loft: that file's
// parse stops reporting well before its end, so an expectation appended there is
// never matched — and an unmatched @EXPECT_ERROR is not a failure, so the test
// would have passed while proving nothing.

PLN119_CONST_TEXT: text = "";

// @EXPECT_ERROR: the left side of this assignment is a value, not a variable
fn pln119_assigning_to_a_file_scope_text_constant() {
  PLN119_CONST_TEXT = "x";
}
